.Dd August 25, 2026
.Dt LANDSTRIP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm landstrip
.Nd run commands inside an OS-level sandbox
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl -debug
.Ar command
.Op Ar options
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
loads one or more policies, applies the current platform sandbox, and runs a
program.
Policies use the supported subset of the Anthropic Sandbox Runtime
format.
JSON is the default; YAML is also accepted.
.Pp
Run
.Nm Ar command Fl -help
for complete command options.
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width "policy validate"
.It Cm run
Run a program under the merged policy.
The
.Fl -
separator before the program is required.
.It Cm policy validate
Check policy parsing, path resolution, executable policy, and platform support.
.It Cm policy resolve
Print the normalized merged policy as JSON.
.It Cm doctor
Print a JSON report showing whether the current sandbox is usable.
.It Cm windows install
Install restricted-user isolation.
This Windows-only command requests elevation.
.It Cm windows status
Report the active Windows implementation and its health.
.It Cm windows uninstall
Remove restricted-user isolation and return to AppContainer.
.El
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -debug
Enable debug logging.
.It Fl p Ar file , Fl -policy Ar file
Read a policy file.
This option may be repeated.
Use
.Sq -
for standard input together with
.Fl -policy-format .
.It Fl -policy-format Ar format
Use
.Va json
or
.Va yaml .
.It Fl -tool Ar program
Include policy attached to the resolved executable when validating or resolving.
.It Fl -trap-fd Ar fd
On Unix, write structured traps to an open descriptor numbered 3 or higher.
.It Fl -restricted-accounts Ar count
Set the restricted-network Windows account pool size.
.It Fl -unrestricted-accounts Ar count
Set the unrestricted-network Windows account pool size.
.It Fl -proxy-port-range Ar low-high
Set the restricted-user loopback proxy range.
.It Fl V , Fl -version
Print the version.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print help.
.El
.Sh POLICY
Later policy files merge over earlier files.
Objects merge recursively, arrays
combine without duplicates, and later scalar values replace earlier values.
Executable policy is merged last.
.Pp
Supported fields include:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{
  "filesystem": {
    "allowWrite": ["."],
    "denyWrite": ["**/.env"],
    "denyRead": ["~/.ssh"],
    "allowRead": ["~/.ssh/config"]
  },
  "network": {
    "allowNetwork": false,
    "httpProxyPort": 8080,
    "socksProxyPort": 8081,
    "allowLocalBinding": false,
    "allowUnixSockets": []
  },
  "windows": {
    "appContainerMode": "lpac",
    "allowLoopback": false
  }
}
.Ed
.Pp
Relative paths resolve from the starting directory.
Tilde paths use the current
user's home directory.
Path entries accept shell-style globs.
.Ss Filesystem
Writes are denied by default.
.Va allowWrite
grants writable roots and
.Va denyWrite
always takes precedence.
Reads remain unrestricted while
.Va denyRead
is empty; otherwise reads use an allowlist and
.Va allowRead
adds exceptions.
.Pp
Concrete write denials are enforced on every platform.
Linux and macOS enforce
glob write denials at access time.
Windows does not enforce write-denial globs.
.Ss Network
Direct TCP connections and new Unix sockets are denied by default.
Proxy port
fields permit their loopback ports but do not start a proxy.
.Va allowLocalBinding
permits local TCP binding and loopback connections.
.Va allowUnixSockets
permits listed pathname sockets.
.Va allowNetwork
turns off TCP enforcement while leaving filesystem policy active.
.Pp
Unix socket policy is unavailable on Windows.
Linux 6.12 and newer also restrict
abstract sockets and signals to processes outside the sandbox.
.Ss Windows
Windows requires an explicit read allowlist.
.Va windows.appContainerMode
selects
.Va lpac
or
.Va standard .
LPAC is the default and stronger boundary.
.Va windows.allowLoopback
exposes all local loopback services in AppContainer mode.
Restricted-user mode
instead permits only the proxy range selected during installation.
.Sh PLATFORM NOTES
.Bl -tag -width Windows
.It Linux
Landlock enforces filesystem roots and a seccomp broker handles network and
query traps.
Sandboxed processes cannot use
.Xr io_uring_setup 2 ,
.Xr io_uring_enter 2 ,
or
.Xr io_uring_register 2 .
Blocking these syscall entry points prevents ring creation and syscall-driven
submission or reconfiguration.
An already-active ring created with
.Dv IORING_SETUP_SQPOLL
and supplied by another process is outside this boundary because it can consume
submissions directly from shared memory.
.It macOS
Seatbelt enforces a profile fixed at process startup.
.It Windows
AppContainer or restricted local users enforce explicit filesystem grants.
Restricted-user mode uses Windows Filtering Platform rules.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT
Policy commands, doctor, and Windows management commands write one JSON document
to standard output.
Failures and completed denials are one-line JSON objects on
standard error with stable kind and code fields.
.Pp
Trap kinds are
.Va filesystem ,
.Va network ,
.Va launch ,
.Va usage ,
and
.Va internal .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm run
returns the sandboxed program's status.
On Linux, policies that require a seccomp broker terminate and reap remaining
descendants when that program exits; their background and daemonized processes
do not outlive the invocation.
Invalid policy validation and unhealthy
doctor or status reports return 1. Other failures return 1 and usage errors
return 2.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Run tests with a JSON policy:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
landstrip run -p policy.json -- cargo test
.Ed
.Pp
Validate YAML from standard input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cat policy.yaml | landstrip policy validate -p - --policy-format yaml
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Lk https://github.com/landstrip/landstrip#readme
